    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">COPD, GOLD grade 2, group B (J44.9)</content>. One moderate exacerbation in the past year keeps him in group B (group E needs two moderate exacerbations or one hospital stay). With eosinophils of 0.08 x 10^3/uL an inhaled corticosteroid is unlikely to help and would raise the risk of pneumonia; azithromycin or roflumilast is for repeated exacerbations despite maintenance therapy. So no change: he stays on the LAMA with LABA. Rehabilitation lengthened his walk from 360 to 425 m and his CAT fell from 18 to 11. [S]<list><item>Continue umeclidinium-vilanterol and albuterol as needed.</item><item>Home exercise program three times a week, with a maintenance class at the rehabilitation center.</item><item>Action plan reviewed: call the clinic the same day for a flare, as he did this time; no standby steroid prescription for now.</item><item>Spirometry and low-dose CT in the summer.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Hyperlipidemia on atorvastatin (E78.5)</content>. Controlled; no muscle symptoms. [S]<list><item>Lipid panel yearly.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Symptoms: CAT and mMRC dyspnea scale</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>CAT 11; mMRC 1</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Exacerbations in the past 12 months (moderate and severe) and GOLD group</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>One moderate exacerbation in the past year; still group B</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Pulse oximetry at rest</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>95 percent on room air</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Spirometry: post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC and FEV1 % predicted (race-neutral GLI Global)</td><td>At diagnosis, then at least yearly or with a clear change</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Next spirometry in the summer, a year after diagnosis</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood eosinophil count</td><td>At baseline and when an inhaled corticosteroid is considered</td><td>Up to date</td><td>0.08 x 10^3/uL at intake; an inhaled corticosteroid considered after the exacerbation and not advised</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Smoking status and cessation support</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Smoke-free</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>BMI and nutrition</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>BMI 22.9; weight steady after the steroid course</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Inhaler technique, adherence and side effects (dry mouth, urinary retention)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Correct technique; no missed doses</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>ECG and cardiovascular risk</td><td>At baseline; reassess after exacerbations</td><td>Deferred</td><td>The ECG that the CMID protocol calls for after an exacerbation was not done at the sick visit. He has had no chest pain or palpitations, and today&apos;s ECG is unchanged from intake, so no harm is evident. Corrective action: ECG done today and added to the clinic&apos;s exacerbation visit template</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Pulmonary rehabilitation</td><td>Offer when mMRC is 2 or more, or CAT 10 or more; within three weeks of a hospital discharge for an exacerbation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Twenty sessions completed five days ago; maintenance class and home program</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Six-minute walk test</td><td>At rehabilitation entry and exit, and for oxygen assessment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>425 m at rehabilitation exit; lowest saturation 92 percent</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Long-term oxygen assessment</td><td>When resting PaO2 is 55 mm Hg or less (60 with cor pulmonale or polycythemia) or saturation 88 percent or less, confirmed twice</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Saturation 95 percent at rest and 92 percent at its lowest on walking</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Written and spoken COPD action plan</td><td>At diagnosis, reviewed after each exacerbation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Used well at the exacerbation; reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Vaccines: influenza, pneumococcal, RSV, COVID-19, Tdap, zoster</td><td>Yearly review</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Influenza, RSV, COVID-19 and the first zoster dose three weeks ago; PCV20 and Tdap at intake; second zoster dose due</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Low-dose CT lung cancer screening</td><td>Yearly at 50 to 80 with 20 or more pack-years, while within fifteen years of quitting</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Next in the summer</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Statin safety: muscle symptoms</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No muscle aches</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood (PHQ-9)</td><td>At intake and when symptoms change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>2, from 5, with item 9 scored 0</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Find the cause of the breathlessness. Outcome by 2026-07-28: COPD confirmed by spirometry (post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC 0.58); heart failure unlikely (NT-proBNP 92, normal ECG); alpha-1 antitrypsin normal [S]</item><item>Walk farther with less breathlessness. Outcome by 2026-10-27: Six-minute walk 360 to 425 m after rehabilitation; CAT 18 to 11 [S]</item><item>Fewer and milder flare-ups, treated early. In progress; target 2027-07-14 [S]</item><item>LDL below 100 mg/dL. Outcome by 2026-09-08: LDL 148 to 86 mg/dL on atorvastatin 20 mg [S]</item><item>Vaccines up to date. Outcome by 2026-10-06: PCV20 and Tdap at intake; influenza, RSV, COVID-19 and the first zoster dose at the wellness visit [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Global Strategy for the Prevention, Diagnosis and Management of COPD: 2025 Report (2025); Race and Ethnicity in Pulmonary Function Test Interpretation: An Official American Thoracic Society Statement (2023); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, COPD and hyperlipidemia monographs (2026); Pulmonary Rehabilitation for Adults with Chronic Respiratory Disease: An Official American Thoracic Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2023); Home Oxygen Therapy for Adults with Chronic Lung Disease: An Official American Thoracic Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2020); A Randomized Trial of Long-Term Oxygen for COPD with Moderate Desaturation (LOTT) (2016); Racial Bias in Pulse Oximetry Measurement (2020); Lung Cancer: Screening (2021); Statin Use for the Primary Prevention of Cardiovascular Disease in Adults: Preventive Medication (2022); Adult Immunization Schedule by Age (2026); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review in three months, sooner for a flare. Spirometry and low-dose CT next summer. Second zoster dose in one to five months. Next wellness visit in a year. [S]</paragraph></text>

